LITERATURE REVIEW AND GAP ANALYSIS
====================================

Research Question:
Can dual-pocket-conditioned de novo molecular generation produce more selective ligands
for structurally similar binding sites compared to single-target generation with subsequent
off-target filtering, without compromising drug-likeness properties?

Date: 2026-02-14
Sources: bioRxiv, ChEMBL, ClinicalTrials.gov, HuggingFace Hub, Web (academic literature)

================================================================================
I. WHAT ALREADY EXISTS IN THE LITERATURE
================================================================================

A. Dual/Multi-Target Generation (Polypharmacology -- Binding BOTH Targets)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

These works generate molecules that bind BOTH targets simultaneously.
This is NOT selectivity -- it is polypharmacology.

1. DiffDTM (2024, IEEE)
   - URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.13957
   - Architecture: Diffusion model, structure-free (protein sequences + molecular graphs)
   - Targets: Dopamine D2R + Serotonin 5-HT1A (antipsychotics)
   - Key: First deep generative model for dual-target molecule generation given
     arbitrary dual targets without retraining. One-shot conditional generation.
   - Validation: In silico (docking, drug-likeness metrics)
   - Limitation: Designed for polypharmacology, NOT selectivity between similar targets.

2. POLYGON (2024, Nature Communications)
   - URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-47120-y
   - Architecture: VAE + Reinforcement Learning
   - Targets: MEK1 + mTOR
   - Key: Embeds chemical space and iteratively samples to generate polypharmacology
     compounds. 82.5% accuracy in recognizing polypharmacology interactions across
     >100K compounds.
   - Validation: 32 compounds synthesized, >50% reduction in both protein activities
     and cell viability at 1-10 uM.
   - Note: Authors state that "including IC50 predictions for a panel of off-target
     proteins as a negative reward" is a FUTURE direction -- confirming selectivity
     is not yet addressed.

3. MTMol-GPT (2024, PLOS Computational Biology)
   - URL: https://journals.plos.org/ploscompbiol/article?id=10.1371/journal.pcbi.1012229
   - Architecture: GPT + Generative Adversarial Imitation Learning
   - Targets: DRD2 + HTR1A
   - Key: Multi-target generation with transformer architecture.
   - Validation: Docking, pharmacophore mapping.

4. AIxFuse (2023, bioRxiv -> Chemical Science)
   - DOI: 10.1101/2023.12.10.571029 (published: 10.1039/D4SC00094C)
   - Architecture: Two self-play RL agents for pharmacophore selection + fusion
   - Targets: GSK3-beta/JNK3, RORgamma-t/DHODH
   - Key: First structure-aware dual-target drug design method. 5x outperformance
     in success rate vs SOTA. Free energy perturbation calculations confirm promising
     binding free energies.
   - Relevance: DIRECTLY addresses dual-pocket structural constraints, but for
     polypharmacology (binding BOTH pockets), not selectivity.

5. EVOSYNTH (2025, bioRxiv)
   - DOI: 10.1101/2025.11.04.686584
   - Architecture: Latent evolution with synthesis-aware prioritization
   - Targets: JNK3/GSK3-beta (Alzheimer's), PI3K/PARP1 (ovarian cancer)
   - Key: Outperforms baseline generative models in predicted affinities, scaffold
     diversity, and lower synthesis costs.

6. DiffMC-Gen (2025, Advanced Science)
   - URL: https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/advs.202417726
   - Architecture: Dual diffusion 2D+3D
   - Key: Multi-conditional molecular generation, but NOT dual-pocket selectivity.

7. MDRL (2025, Journal of Cheminformatics)
   - URL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13321-025-01035-y
   - Architecture: 3D diffusion + reinforcement learning
   - Key: Joint optimization of multi-target binding and molecular properties.
     Polypharmacology focus, not selectivity.


B. Selectivity via Post-Hoc Optimization (Single-Target + Filtering/Optimization)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. DiffSBDD -- Negative Design (2024, Nature Computational Science)
   - URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s43588-024-00737-x
   - PMC: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11659159/
   - Architecture: SE(3)-equivariant diffusion model
   - Strategy: Explicit negative design via modified SAMPLING (not retraining).
     Iterative noising/denoising + simultaneous scoring against on-target and off-target.
   - Targets: BIKE kinase (on-target, PDB 4w9w) vs MPSK1 kinase (off-target, PDB 2buj)
   - Results:
     * On-target docking score: -7.2 -> -13.9 (5 rounds of optimization)
     * Off-target docking score: -10.8 -> -8.7
     * "Substantially improved specificity"
   - Key insight: A single pretrained diffusion model can perform property optimization,
     explicit negative design, and partial molecular design (inpainting) by changing
     only the sampling strategy.
   - Limitation: Selectivity is achieved POST-HOC via iterative optimization, NOT
     through a priori dual-pocket conditioning during generation.

2. CMD-GEN -- Pharmacophore Matching (2025, Communications Biology)
   - URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s42003-025-07840-3
   - Architecture: Coarse-grained pharmacophore diffusion model
   - Strategy: Bridges ligand-protein complexes with drug-like molecules using
     pharmacophore points. Compares pharmacophore clouds between target and anti-target.
   - Targets: PARP1 vs PARP2
   - Results: >787-fold selectivity for PARP1 over PARP2
   - Validation: WET-LAB confirmed
   - Key: Closest to structural comparison of two pockets, but selectivity through
     pharmacophore DIFFERENCE analysis, not simultaneous conditioning.

3. BInD -- NCI Pattern Guidance (2025, Advanced Science)
   - URL: https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/advs.202502702
   - Architecture: Bond and Interaction-generating Diffusion model
   - Strategy: Non-covalent interaction (NCI) patterns as knowledge-based guidance
   - Targets: EGFR double-mutant vs wild-type
   - Results: Median affinity difference 0.69 kcal/mol for mutant EGFR
   - CRITICAL FINDING: Tested TargetDiff and Pocket2Mol -- BOTH FAILED to produce
     selective molecules (no significant affinity differences between pockets).
     This directly proves that current pocket-conditioned models lack selectivity.

4. DrugHIVE -- Evolutionary Latent Space Search (2024, JCIM)
   - URL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jcim.4c01193
   - PMC: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11350878/
   - Architecture: Deep hierarchical VAE
   - Strategy: Evolutionary algorithm optimizing selectivity ratio in latent space.
     Generate for target first, then optimize against anti-target.
   - Targets: ATM kinase vs Vps34 kinase
   - Results: Best molecule ATM -9.3 kcal/mol vs Vps34 -6.1 kcal/mol
   - Metrics evaluated: Vina docking, QED, SA, ALogP, molecular diversity (Tanimoto)
   - Limitation: Sequential optimization, not simultaneous dual-pocket conditioning.

5. CogMol -- Multi-Attribute Sampling (2020, NeurIPS)
   - URL: https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/2d16ad1968844a4300e9a490588ff9f8-Abstract.html
   - Architecture: VAE + CLaSS (Controlled Latent attribute Space Sampling)
   - Strategy: Multi-attribute controlled sampling including off-target selectivity
     as an explicit attribute alongside QED and SA.
   - Targets: Three SARS-CoV-2 targets (Mpro, spike RBD, NSP9)
   - Results: 87-95% of high-affinity molecules showed docking <-6 kcal/mol.
     Novel at molecular and scaffold levels.
   - Key: Early work explicitly incorporating off-target selectivity as a
     generation attribute, but NOT structure-based pocket conditioning.

6. DRAGONFLY (2024, Nature Communications)
   - URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-47613-w
   - Architecture: Graph-to-sequence deep learning, interactome-based
   - Strategy: Two scoring procedures: (1) selective single-target PPARgamma,
     (2) dual PPARgamma+PPARdelta.
   - Results: Potent PPARgamma partial agonists with desired selectivity profiles.
     Crystal structure confirmed binding mode.
   - Validation: WET-LAB (synthesis + crystal structure)

7. REACTOR (2021, ACS Omega)
   - Architecture: RL with chemical reactions as MDP transitions
   - Strategy: Off-target penalty in multi-objective reward
   - Targets: DRD2 vs D1/D3 receptors
   - Key: Maintains higher number of desirable molecules on selectivity tasks.

8. Entangled AAE (2020, cited in 2024 reviews)
   - Architecture: Semi-supervised Adversarial Autoencoder
   - Strategy: Conditional generation with low/high activity settings per target
   - Targets: JAK2 vs JAK3
   - Results: One molecule synthesized with confirmed in vitro selectivity for JAK3.

9. Selective Kinase Design via MCTS (2022, JCIM)
   - URL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jcim.2c00787
   - Architecture: ChemTS (RNN + Monte Carlo Tree Search)
   - Strategy: 18 simultaneous objectives including 9 tyrosine kinase activities +
     pharmacokinetic + synthesizability + drug-likeness
   - Key: Multi-objective optimization framing of selectivity.


C. Pocket-Conditioned Generation for Selectivity (Experimental Validation)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. ATOM-GMD -- Generative Molecular Design with Selectivity Validation (2023, bioRxiv)
   - DOI: 10.1101/2023.02.14.528391
   - From: Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory
   - Targets: Histamine H1 (on-target) vs M2 receptor (off-target)
   - Results: 6 compounds binding H1 with Ki 10-100 nM, at least 100-fold selective
     over M2 receptors. 103 compounds synthesized and tested.
   - Key: One of the few papers with WET-LAB selectivity confirmation.

2. SE3-BiLingoMol (2025, bioRxiv)
   - DOI: 10.1101/2025.09.23.678079
   - Architecture: SE(3)-equivariant Transformer for pocket-based 3D generation
   - Targets: HPK1 (selective inhibitor)
   - Results: Potent and selective HPK1 inhibitor with IN VIVO efficacy
   - Key: Very recent, demonstrating pocket-conditioned generation achieving
     selectivity with in vivo validation.

3. Multi-objective cGVAE (2018, Journal of Cheminformatics)
   - URL: https://jcheminf.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13321-018-0287-6
   - Strategy: Conditional generation with selectivity conditioning codes
   - Targets: JNK3-selective, GSK3beta-selective, or dual inhibitors
   - Key: Early foundational work on selectivity-conditioned generation.


D. Single-Pocket Conditioned Models (Context -- No Selectivity)
---------------------------------------------------------------

All of these are single-pocket models without selectivity capabilities:

- PMDM (2024, Nature Communications): Dual equivariant encoders (local+global dynamics).
  URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-46569-1
- PILOT (2024, Chemical Science): Equivariant diffusion + multi-objective importance sampling.
  URL: https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlehtml/2024/sc/d4sc03523b
- DiffGui (2025, Nature Communications): Target-conditioned with property guidance.
  URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-63245-0
- MolCRAFT (2024): First SBDD in continuous parameter space. Vina -6.59 kcal/mol.
- MolJO (2024): SOTA on CrossDocked2020 (Success Rate 51.3%, Vina Dock -9.05).
- DrugFlow (2025): Flow matching + discrete Markov bridges.
- DrugGPS (2023): Subpocket prototypes for generalizable SBDD.
- DecompOpt (2024): Decomposed diffusion for structure-based optimization.
- Apo2Mol (2025): Accounts for protein conformational flexibility.
- 3DMolFormer (2025): Unified dual-channel transformer for docking and drug design.
- FREED++ (2024): RL-based fragment generation. No selectivity component.
- MolSnapper (2024): Conditioning diffusion for SBDD.
  URL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jcim.4c02008


E. Benchmarks and Evaluation Frameworks
-----------------------------------------

1. MolScore (2024, Journal of Cheminformatics) -- MOST RELEVANT
   - URL: https://jcheminf.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s13321-024-00861-w
   - Includes 5-HT2A selectivity benchmarks with dual docking
   - Three difficulty levels: PhysChem, QSAR, dual molecular docking
   - Critical finding: Selectivity optimization is significantly harder than
     single-target -- selectivity "barely improved" during optimization.
   - Presets: '5HT2A_PhysChem', '5HT2A_Selectivity', '5HT2A_Docking'

2. CrossDocked2020 -- Standard SBDD Benchmark
   - ~22.5M poses, ~19,443 protein-ligand complexes
   - NO selectivity evaluation metrics
   - Used by: DiffSBDD, TargetDiff, Pocket2Mol, PMDM, MolCRAFT, MolJO, etc.

3. DOCKSTRING -- Docking Score Benchmark
   - Includes selectivity tasks: JAK2 vs LCK
   - One of the few benchmarks with explicit selectivity objectives.

4. POKMOL-3D (2024, JCIM)
   - URL: https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jcim.4c01598
   - 32 protein targets, new 2D/3D evaluation metrics
   - Single-target only, no selectivity.

5. SBDD Benchmark: Do 3D Methods Really Dominate? (2024, arXiv)
   - URL: https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.03403
   - Cross-algorithm comparison of 16 models

6. 3D SBDD: Are We There Yet? (2024, bioRxiv)
   - DOI: 10.1101/2024.12.27.629537
   - Novel metrics for chemical plausibility
   - Shows current algorithms generate chemically implausible structures

7. "Is Structure-Based Drug Design Ready for Selectivity Optimization?" (2020, JCIM)
   - URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8310368/
   - FEP for selectivity CDK2/CDK9 and CDK2/ERK2
   - Key insight: Binding site similarity may lead to cancellation of errors,
     allowing selectivity to be predicted MORE accurately than affinity.

8. Scoring Function Concern -- Boltz-2 (2025)
   - URL: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12262699/
   - Independent assessment found affinity predictions "insensitive to key
     binding site mutations and even in some cases to target exchange"
   - Raises concerns about using AI scoring for selectivity evaluation.


================================================================================
II. ChEMBL DATA: TARGET FAMILIES WITH SELECTIVITY DATA
================================================================================

A. Target Pairs with Rich Selectivity Data
--------------------------------------------

JAK Family (Gold Standard):
- JAK1: CHEMBL2835 (7,342 IC50 with pChEMBL >= 7)
- JAK2: CHEMBL2971 (8,030 IC50 with pChEMBL >= 7)
- JAK3: CHEMBL2148
- TYK2: CHEMBL3553
- Total: >15,000 high-quality IC50 measurements across family
- PDB coverage: JAK1 50+ structures, JAK2 130+ structures
- Best selective drug: Upadacitinib (JAK1/JAK2: 3x biochemical, 75x cellular)

CDK Family (Unmet Need):
- CDK4: CHEMBL331 (13 PDB structures)
- CDK6: CHEMBL2508 (19 PDB structures)
- CDK4/CDK6 selectivity is practically UNACHIEVABLE with current approaches
- Palbociclib is a dual CDK4/6 inhibitor (>100x selective over CDK2)
- PERFECT test case for dual-pocket methods

PI3K Family (Clinical Validation):
- PI3Ka: CHEMBL4005 (90+ PDB structures)
- PI3Kd: CHEMBL3130 (17 PDB structures)
- Alpelisib: PI3Ka-selective (>10x)
- Idelalisib: PI3Kd-selective (>50x)

EGFR (Mutant Selectivity):
- CHEMBL203 (extensive PDB coverage, 10,000+ IC50)
- Mutant vs WT selectivity clinically validated

B. Key Selectivity Ratios Achieved by Existing Drugs
------------------------------------------------------

Drug              | Target Pair      | Selectivity Ratio              | Notes
Upadacitinib      | JAK1/JAK2        | 3x biochemical, 75x cellular   | Best-in-class JAK1
Tofacitinib       | JAK3/JAK1        | ~100x                          | Poor JAK2 selectivity
Alpelisib         | PI3Ka/other PI3K | >10x                           | Isoform-selective
Idelalisib        | PI3Kd/other PI3K | >50x                           | Leukocyte-restricted
Palbociclib       | CDK4,6/CDK2      | >100x                          | Dual, selective vs CDK2
Imatinib          | ABL/broader kinome| Selective for defined set      | Multi-kinase

C. Structural Insights
------------------------
- JAK family: All 4 members have ATP-binding sites with high structural conservation.
  Selectivity exploits subtle differences in gatekeeper residues and glycine-rich loop.
- PI3K family: Alpha and delta share lipid kinase fold but differ in loop conformations.
- CDK family: CDK4 and CDK6 have NEARLY IDENTICAL binding sites -- exemplar case for
  dual-pocket methods.


