III. CLINICAL TRIALS: SELECTIVITY-DRIVEN DRUG CANDIDATES
================================================================================

A. Mutant-Selective PI3Ka Inhibitors -- Most Relevant Clinical Class
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Compound    | Sponsor             | Phase   | NCT ID         | Status         | N
RLY-2608    | Relay Therapeutics  | Phase 3 | NCT06982521    | Recruiting     | 540
RLY-2608    | Relay Therapeutics  | Phase 1 | NCT05216432    | Recruiting     | 930
STX-478     | Eli Lilly           | Phase 1/2| NCT05768139   | Recruiting     | 720
OKI-219     | OnKure              | Phase 1 | NCT06239467    | Recruiting     | 200
CGT6297     | Cogent Biosciences  | Phase 1 | NCT07383506    | Not recruiting | 90
ETX-636     | Ensem Therapeutics  | Phase 1/2| NCT06993844   | Recruiting     | 233
RLY-5836    | Relay Therapeutics  | Phase 1 | NCT05759949    | Completed      | 41

RLY-2608 is arguably the MOST ADVANCED computationally designed selectivity-optimized
drug in clinical development. Designed using Relay's motion-based drug design platform
(Dynamo). Selective for mutant PI3Ka over wild-type, reducing hyperglycemia/metabolic
toxicities of first-gen inhibitors.

B. AI Drug Discovery Companies -- Clinical Pipeline
-----------------------------------------------------

Insilico Medicine (9 trials):
- INS018_055 (Rentosertib): First AI-discovered drug in Phase 2 (COMPLETED with results)
  NCT05938920. Target, compound, and indication all identified by AI.
- ISM3412, ISM8207, ISM6331, ISM5411, ISM5939: Phase 1 trials

Recursion/Exscientia (8 trials):
- GTAEXS617 (REC-617): AI-designed CDK7 inhibitor, Phase 1/2, n=230
  NCT05985655. Selectivity as key design criterion.
- EXS21546: AI-designed A2A receptor antagonist, Phase 1 (completed)
- EXS73565, REC-4881, REC-1245: Phase 1-2

Relay Therapeutics (5 trials): See mutant-selective PI3Ka above.

C. Other Selective Inhibitors in Clinical Trials
--------------------------------------------------
- Avapritinib (BLU-285): Selective KIT D816V mutant TKI (NCT03731260)
- Acalabrutinib: Selective BTK inhibitor (NCT03198650)
- Abrocitinib + Ritlecitinib: JAK1/JAK3-selective for T1D (NCT05743244)
- Selective CDK inhibitor P1446A-05: Phase 1 completed

D. Key Clinical Insight
-------------------------
125+ trials explicitly reference selective inhibitors for cancer alone.
64 involve selective kinase inhibitors in Phase 1-3.
Mutant-selective PI3Ka inhibitors are the clearest proof that
selectivity-by-design translates to improved therapeutic windows.
ClinicalTrials.gov does NOT capture computational methods in structured fields --
the computational origin is only discoverable by knowing sponsor companies.


================================================================================
IV. HUGGINGFACE HUB: ML/AI LANDSCAPE
================================================================================

A. Key SBDD Models (from HF Papers)
--------------------------------------

No models for multi-pocket/selectivity-aware generation exist on HuggingFace.
The field's code primarily lives on GitHub.

Established single-pocket-conditioned methods:
- MolCRAFT (2024): First SBDD in continuous parameter space
- MolJO (2024): SOTA on CrossDocked2020 (SR 51.3%, Vina -9.05)
- DrugFlow (2025): Flow matching + discrete Markov bridges
- DrugGPS (2023): Subpocket prototypes -- closest conceptual precursor to
  dual-pocket methods
- DecompOpt (2024): Decomposed diffusion for fine-grained control
- Apo2Mol (2025): Protein flexibility-aware generation
- 3DMolFormer (2025): Unified dual-channel transformer
- FuncBind (2025): Neural fields for all-atom generation
- SynCoGen (2025): Synthesizable 3D generation

Docking and scoring:
- DiffDock (2022): 38% top-1 success on PDBBind
- FlowDock (2024): 51% blind docking success, Top-5 at CASP16
- PoseX (2025): 22 methods compared, AI surpasses physics-based

B. Key Gap
-----------
ALL current SBDD models condition on a SINGLE pocket. No model jointly conditions
on two pockets (on-target + off-target) to optimize selectivity.

Closest building blocks:
- DrugGPS subpocket prototypes could contrast on-target vs off-target subpockets
- DecompOpt decomposed approach enables fine-grained R-group control
- MolJO multi-objective optimization could add selectivity objectives
- Apo2Mol flexibility awareness relevant for induced-fit selectivity differences


================================================================================
V. CRITICAL GAP ANALYSIS
================================================================================

A. What NOBODY Has Done
-------------------------

1. NO HEAD-TO-HEAD COMPARISON of dual-pocket conditioning vs single-target +
   off-target filtering on the same benchmark with the same metrics.

2. NO MODEL WITH A PRIORI DUAL-POCKET CONDITIONING:
   - DiffSBDD: negative design post-hoc (via modified sampling)
   - DrugHIVE: sequential generate-then-optimize
   - CMD-GEN: pharmacophore difference analysis
   - BInD: NCI pattern guidance
   - NONE conditions on both pockets simultaneously during generation

3. NO STANDARDIZED SELECTIVITY BENCHMARK:
   - CrossDocked2020 has no selectivity metrics
   - MolScore has only 5-HT2A/D2
   - No multi-family benchmark exists

4. BInD PROVED THAT EXISTING MODELS FAIL AT SELECTIVITY:
   - TargetDiff: no significant affinity differences between pockets
   - Pocket2Mol: no significant affinity differences between pockets
   - This is DIRECT MOTIVATION for new approaches

5. DRUG-LIKENESS TRADE-OFF NOT STUDIED:
   - Unknown how dual conditioning affects QED/SA/Lipinski
   - Unknown if constraining to two pockets causes mode collapse or
     reduces molecular diversity

6. LIMITED WET-LAB VALIDATION:
   - Only CMD-GEN (PARP1/2) and DRAGONFLY (PPARg) have prospective
     selectivity validation
   - Only ATOM-GMD has systematic selectivity measurement (H1 vs M2)


================================================================================
VI. VERDICT: CAN YOU WRITE THIS PAPER?
================================================================================

YES -- this is a strong, original, and publishable research question.

Reasons:
1. The question is PRECISELY NOT ANSWERED in existing literature.
   Dual-pocket conditioning for selectivity (not polypharmacology) with
   direct comparison vs single-target+filtering is a clear gap.

2. Components EXIST but are NOT CONNECTED:
   - DiffSBDD can do negative design
   - DiffDTM can do dual-target conditioning
   - POLYGON can do multi-target RL
   - BInD proved existing models fail at selectivity
   - Nobody has combined these insights into a head-to-head comparison

3. The DualDocking project infrastructure (docking, scoring, analysis pipeline)
   is directly applicable.

4. Clinical relevance is high: RLY-2608 (Phase 3) proves selectivity-by-design
   translates to better drugs.


================================================================================
VII. PROPOSED EXPERIMENTAL PLAN
================================================================================

A. Selectivity Benchmark (Novel Contribution)
-----------------------------------------------

Build a benchmark from 4-6 pairs of structurally similar targets:

Target Pair     | ChEMBL IDs          | PDB Structures  | Clinical Relevance
JAK1/JAK2       | CHEMBL2835/2971     | 50+/130+        | Upadacitinib (3x/75x)
CDK4/CDK6       | CHEMBL331/2508      | 13/19           | Unmet need (no selective drugs)
PI3Ka/PI3Kd     | CHEMBL4005/3130     | 90+/17          | Alpelisib/Idelalisib
EGFR WT/mutant  | CHEMBL203           | Extensive        | BInD already tested
PARP1/PARP2     | --                  | Available        | CMD-GEN already tested (787x)
D2R/D3R         | --                  | Available        | Classic GPCR selectivity

B. Methods to Compare (3 Paradigms)
--------------------------------------

Baseline A: Single-pocket generation (TargetDiff/DiffSBDD/MolCRAFT) +
            post-hoc docking filter against off-target

Baseline B: DiffSBDD negative design (iterative noising/denoising + dual scoring)

Proposed:   True dual-pocket conditioned generation (conditioning on target +
            anti-target pockets during generation process itself)

C. Metrics (Comprehensive)
----------------------------

Category      | Metrics
Selectivity   | Delta Vina score, selectivity ratio, hit rate (% > threshold)
Drug-likeness | QED, SA, Lipinski compliance, logP
Quality       | Validity, uniqueness, novelty, PoseBusters pass rate
Diversity     | Tanimoto distance, scaffold diversity
Efficiency    | Time per molecule, compute cost

D. Wet-Lab Validation (for High-Impact Publication)
------------------------------------------------------
- Synthesize 10-20 top candidates for 1-2 target pairs
- Binding assay on-target + off-target
- Selectivity index measurement
- Crystal structure (if possible) for top selective compound

E. Ablation Studies
---------------------
- Dual conditioning vs single conditioning + filter
- Effect of pocket similarity on dual conditioning performance
- Drug-likeness distribution comparison across methods
- Computational efficiency comparison


